# `dist/` is a generated bundle, and git is perfectly happy to three-way merge one.
#
# Two branches that each ran `npm run build` produce edits all over it, mostly in
# different places — so a merge or a rebase splices them into a single file that
# corresponds to neither build, and does it silently, with no conflict to notice.
# That file is what the action actually runs, so the result is a release nobody
# compiled. The CI staleness check catches it on the next push; it does not catch
# it at the moment it is created.
#
# Unsetting `merge` makes git stop and declare a conflict instead. Nothing is
# hand-resolved when it does — the bundle is an artifact, not source: rebuild it
# with `npm run build` and stage the result.
/dist/** -merge
